Computation Structures — MIT 6.004 Spring 2017 (@computing-source-structures: 0, is #reference) { r_08E48B371DEF44C3F62A70A8CB
Chris Terman. Computation Structures, MIT 6.004, Spring 2017. Undergraduate course published by MIT OpenCourseWare. [Course and topic sequence](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/) · [Syllabus](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/syllabus/).

For the current common block study the annotated explanations and appropriate introductory exercises in topics 1 (information and binary representation), 4 (combinational logic), 5 (sequential logic) and 9 (instruction-set design). Introduce the necessary digital-abstraction context from topic 2; detailed CMOS device work and unfamiliar circuit timing require later preparation. The complete 21-topic course, laboratories and design project supply a larger later formation through procedures, processor implementation, memory, virtualization, devices and concurrency. Assigning them does not claim they were completed.

On 31 October 2026, instructor, term, level, syllabus and topic organization were inspected, together with selected annotated passages in [1.1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c1/c1s1/), [4.1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c4/c4s1/), [5.1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c5/c5s1/) and [9.1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c9/c9s1/). This is bounded inspection of representation, truth-table, stored-state/timing and instruction/memory discussions, not a complete slide, video, worksheet or laboratory audit. The course’s historical hardware sizes/performance and simplified explanatory claims are not presented as current universal device specifications.

The site’s Creative Commons link identifies CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Preserve separately credited component terms and the OCW terms of use before reuse; access to the course does not imply an unrestricted licence for every embedded work. Slides, images, videos, worksheets and software remain external. The new four-bit machine, byte encoding, sum program and checks are original constructions; they do not implement the course’s Beta architecture or reproduce its laboratory results.

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).

**Fourth computing-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected official annotated passages in [topic 12 procedures](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c12/c12s1/), [topic 14 memory hierarchy](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c14/c14s1/), [topic 16 virtual memory](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c16/c16s1/) and [topic 17 virtualizing the processor](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c17/c17s1/) were inspected for nested return-state preservation, hit/miss cost, page/offset translation and permissions, process context and controlled or blocked services. Add these topics as progressive common reading. The larger course, full videos, exercises and architecture-specific implementation laboratories were not newly audited or performed. Historical hardware costs and the course architecture are not current universal specifications; the new finite maps, service transitions, cache costs and two-process programs are original bounded teaching models. Prior source identity, assignments and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0/component terms remain; the official rights link was rechecked. Keep all course media external.

